                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is one of those games that I bought over a decade ago, forgot about, and never played. I finally gave it a go, and well, it was... interesting, to say the least. Did I like it? Kind of. I didn't dislike it per say, just because, well, there's not much here to dislike. This is a very short game with minimalistic controls, and not  much substance. This is technically what I think most people would consider to be a shit game. There's not really all that much to do, it's not very scary, it's got a convoluted story with no real character interactions, no combat, no character progression or leveling, and its puzzles are not very complex or thought provoking. Your character is never in any real danger. There are a handful of jump scares but none of them are all that frightening, none of them made me jump. \n\nThis is a very weird game that I probably wouldn't recommend to almost anyone, because it's so niche, but I enjoyed it for what it was. It is basically a horror walking-simulator that tells its story via notes that you find laying around in the game's environment. It has a dreamlike structure in which the character moves through spaces which do not really make sense, but, it's not really supposed to make sense. It's more about the vibe. Walking simulator type games are just like that, I guess. If you're down for that, well, that's exactly what you're getting yourself into. \n\nAs far as the story is concerned, from what I understood, you're like some sort of detective, like a supernatural detective or something like that, and you have like, files or something, about these evil psychopathic killers, and you have to delve into their psychology or something like that? I really don't understand it to be honest. I don't think you're really supposed to understand it. It's pretty out there. \n\nIs it worth ten dollars? Hell no LMAO. Especially considering that this is split into three games, of which this only contains the first two chapters. The second game apparently only contains the third chapter. I own the second one but apparently never bought the third one, so, I'll go ahead and give chapter three a shot now. Get this on sale, I'd say it's worth maybe three or four dollars at the most. Based on the Steam completion rate, it seems like the vast majority of players who played this game never even beat chapter one, so take that for what you will. \n\nThe game is extremely dark by the way, so it's nice to look at on an OLED monitor. If you don't have one, I'd probably save up for one of those before you spend any money on this game, honestly. \n\nSomething out of ten? I'm confused not gonna lie.",
